@using SchoolCollab.Admin.Shared.Constants
@* PanelSection (owner, 2026-10-10) — the ONE box on a create / edit surface
   (documents/solution/panel-layout-pattern.md §3). Every block on those surfaces wears it: the
   assignment authoring surface's Details, Questions & AI, Targets & Audience, Content & Resources
   and Rules, and the notification-template editor's Message, Constraints and Preview.

   NAME: deliberately `PanelSection`, not `Panel` — `Panel` is exactly the kind of name FluentUI is
   likely to introduce, and a bare `Panel` in this namespace would then collide or shadow. (The
   component was written as `TitledSection`, briefly `Panel`, and settled here.)

   ITS RULE — kept here because it is the thing most easily lost in a refactor:
   **a THIN LINE runs underneath the header, spanning the FULL header row — the title AND the
   `HeaderActions` slot (the kebab menu, the page action) — not just the width of the title text.**
   It is a sibling of the header row (not a child of the title), full-width inside the panel's own
   box, with the margin that separates the header from the content beneath. Owner-corrected
   2026-10-10: the first pass drew it as the title's own border, which stopped short of the kebab.

   THE HEAD GROUP: the header row and its rule are wrapped in ONE element so the rule can never
   scroll away from the title it belongs to. Sticky was REMOVED from this component on 2026-10-24
   (owner, #404): the page action header owns pinning now, and two stacked pinned rows was the
   overlap the owner reported. A panel header is a label, not a second toolbar.

   COLLAPSIBILITY IS OPT-IN (owner, 2026-10-23; §6). `Collapsible` defaults to false and a false
   panel renders **no chevron element at all** — not a disabled one, not a CSS-hidden one. Showing a
   control that looks collapsible and is not is worse than showing nothing.

   THE BORDER IS OPT-OUT (owner, 2026-10-24; §3). A panel is a box by default. The MAIN region's
   blocks turn the box off (`ShowBorder="false"`) and are separated from their header by the header
   rule alone; the rail's blocks keep the box, because a stack of unboxed panels in a narrow column
   has no edge to read. The rule is `border-color: transparent` — never `border: none` — so the two
   variants keep the same box model and switching one does not shift the layout by a pixel. *@
<div class="panel-section @(ShowBorder ? null : "panel-section--borderless")" id="@Id">
    @if (ShowHeader)
    {
    <div class="panel-section__head">
        <div class="panel-section__header">
            @* The chevron LEADS: it is part of the title's affordance, not a trailing control
               (a trailing chevron was proposed and rejected by the owner, 2026-10-23). *@
            @if (Collapsible)
            {
                <button type="button"
                        class="panel-section__chevron"
                        aria-expanded="@ExpandedAttribute"
                        aria-controls="@_bodyId"
                        aria-label="@ChevronLabel"
                        @onclick="Toggle">
                    <FluentIcon Value="@ChevronIcon" />
                </button>
            }
            <h4 class="panel-section__title" id="@TitleId">@Title</h4>
            @* The count belongs to the TITLE, not to the actions block (§6 header order:
               `[chevron] [Title] [count] … [kebab]`). It sat inside HeaderActions until 2026-10-24, which
               put it at the far right of the row — the title's `flex: 1 1 auto` pushed the whole actions
               wrapper away, so "Guardians" and its "0" read as unrelated (owner report). *@
            @if (Count is { } count)
            {
                <span class="panel-section__count" id="@CountId">@count</span>
            }
            @if (HeaderActions is not null)
            {
                <div class="panel-section__actions">@HeaderActions</div>
            }
        </div>

        <span class="panel-section__rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </div>
    }

    @* Collapsing hides the BODY, never the panel: title, count, chevron and kebab stay visible, so
       a relationship's existence is never a click away. The body element is ALWAYS rendered and
       merely marked `hidden` while collapsed — that keeps the chevron's `aria-controls` pointing at
       an element that exists (a dangling reference announces nothing to a screen reader) and keeps
       the children's state across a collapse. *@
    @* A hidden header cannot host the chevron, so a headerless panel is always expanded — otherwise the
       body would be collapsed with no affordance that could ever reopen it. *@
    <div class="panel-section__body" id="@_bodyId" hidden="@(ShowHeader && !_expanded)">@ChildContent</div>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The panel's own id (the bUnit seam) — optional, because a caller may already own one
    /// (the Content &amp; Resources block keeps `authoring-materials-section`; the assignment
    /// surface's first block keeps `authoring-basics`).</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Id { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The header text. Title Case, like every other header on the authoring surface.
    /// <para>
    /// OPTIONAL (§4): the component can render an untitled box. In practice every block on the
    /// create / edit surfaces is titled — an untitled box makes the reader infer the block's
    /// subject — so the optionality is a capability, not an invitation.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Title { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The id for the title element itself, so an assertion can address the TEXT and the
    /// actions separately — they are distinct elements, the rule the owner asked to keep.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? TitleId { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Right-hand header slot: the kebab menu, the page action, a card's Add — anything on
    /// the header line that is not the title. The rule beneath is drawn under THIS too.</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? HeaderActions { get; set; }

    /// <summary>A count chip rendered immediately after the title (§6 header order:
    /// <c>[chevron] [Title] [count] … [kebab]</c>). Null renders nothing — a panel whose body holds one
    /// record, or none, must not show a count. Pass <see cref="CountId"/> when a test or a caller needs a
    /// stable seam on the chip.</summary>
    [Parameter] public int? Count { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The count chip's element id. Optional; null renders the chip without one.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? CountId { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the panel renders its header row (title, count, actions and the rule) at all.
    /// <b>True by default.</b>
    /// <para>
    /// Set it to <see langword="false"/> only where the block's subject is already unambiguous from the
    /// page itself — the create forms, where the page title and the single visible block say the same
    /// thing (owner, 2026-10-24). A hidden header renders no DOM at all: no empty row, no orphan rule,
    /// and no <c>HeaderActions</c> slot. Do not use it to "tidy" a block whose subject a reader would
    /// otherwise have to infer — the title is what names the block.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool ShowHeader { get; set; } = true;

    /// <summary>
    /// Opt-in collapse (§6): when true the header carries a chevron button immediately before the
    /// title and the body collapses to the header row. Defaults to <b>false</b> — an opt-in default
    /// means a caller has to ask for the affordance, and forgetting to ask can never silently add a
    /// control the owner did not consent to. The main region is never collapsible.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Collapsible { get; set; }


    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the block draws its frame (§3). Defaults to <b>true</b> — a panel is a box unless the
    /// caller says otherwise. The <b>main region's</b> blocks pass <c>false</c>: that region is the
    /// page's own content, not a card, and its separation comes from the header rule
    /// (<see cref="Title"/> sits above a full-width bottom border). The <b>rail's</b> blocks keep the
    /// box — a narrow column of unboxed blocks reads as one undifferentiated list.
    /// Hidden via <c>border-color: transparent</c>, so both variants occupy identical space.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool ShowBorder { get; set; } = true;

    /// <summary>The panel body. Wrap it in `<ChildContent>` whenever <see cref="HeaderActions"/>
    /// is also supplied (RZ9996).</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Collapse state is per panel and NOT persisted: a re-render keeps it, a remount
    /// returns to expanded, so a user never has to remember what they hid. Collapsible panels start
    /// expanded.</summary>
    private bool _expanded = true;

    /// <summary>The body's id, the target of the chevron's `aria-controls`. Derived from
    /// <see cref="Id"/> when the caller owns one, so it is stable across renders of this instance
    /// and readable in a test; a per-instance sequence number keeps untitled-Id panels unique.</summary>
    private string _bodyId = default!;

    private static int _sequence;

    /// <summary>`aria-expanded` as the string the attribute carries.</summary>
    private string ExpandedAttribute => _expanded ? "true" : "false";

    /// <summary>The chevron's accessible name — the action it performs on the panel it names.</summary>
    private string ChevronLabel => (_expanded ? "Collapse " : "Expand ") + (Title ?? "panel");

    /// <summary>Leading chevron: pointing down while expanded (the body is open), right while
    /// collapsed (the title it opens is to its right), the console mock's own reading.</summary>
    private Icon ChevronIcon => _expanded
        ? SchoolCollab.Admin.Shared.Constants.FluentIcons.ChevronDown
        : SchoolCollab.Admin.Shared.Constants.FluentIcons.ChevronRight;

    protected override void OnInitialized() =>
        _bodyId = (Id is { Length: > 0 } id ? id : $"panel-section-{Interlocked.Increment(ref _sequence)}") + "-body";

    private void Toggle() => _expanded = !_expanded;
}
